1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,960
Imagine a hotel with rooms numbered one,
two, three, and so on without end.

2
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:09,520
Every room has one guest.

3
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:11,320
A new guest arrives.

4
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:13,080
Can everyone have a room?

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:22,470
Assign the guest in room one to room two,
the guest in room two to

6
00:00:22,470 --> 00:00:27,740
room three, and in general the guest in
room n to room n plus one.

7
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:31,200
Now assign the new guest room one.

8
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:34,300
Every original guest has a distinct room.

9
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:40,630
The new guest has one too, and every room
is assigned: room one to the newcomer,

10
00:00:40,630 --> 00:00:46,480
and room k, for k at least two, to the
former guest from room k minus one.

11
00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:49,953
That is impossible for a finite hotel.

12
00:00:49,953 --> 00:00:53,064
For an infinite set, a one-to-one
matching can

13
00:00:53,064 --> 00:00:56,313
pair the whole set with a proper part of
itself.

14
00:00:56,313 --> 00:00:59,050
This is a mathematical assignment, not a

15
00:00:59,050 --> 00:01:02,333
practical plan for moving infinitely many
people.

